{"name":"Cricket","hex":"#a6a081","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cricket","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An insect in the order Orthoptera, especially family Gryllidae, that makes a chirping sound by rubbing its wing casings against combs on its hind legs.\n In the form crickets: absolute silence; no communication.","In the form crickets: absolute silence; no communication."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A game played outdoors with bats and a ball between two teams of eleven, popular in England and many Commonwealth countries.","An act that is fair and sportsmanlike."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cricket"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cricket","blocks":[{"text":"Cricket is a bat-and-ball game that is played between two teams of eleven players on a field, at the centre of which is a 22-yard pitch with a wicket at each end, each comprising two bails balanced on three stumps. Two players from the batting team, the striker and nonstriker, stand in front of either wicket holding bats, while one player from the fielding team, the bowler, bowls the ball toward the striker's wicket from the opposite end of the pitch. The striker's goal is to hit the bowled ball with the bat and then switch places with the nonstriker, with the batting team scoring one run for each of these swaps. Runs are also scored when the ball reaches the boundary of the field or when the ball is bowled illegally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Pollock_to_Hussey.jpg/330px-Pollock_to_Hussey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket"},{"title":"Cricket (insect)","blocks":[{"text":"Crickets are orthopteran insects which are related to bush crickets and, more distantly, to grasshoppers. In older literature, such as Imms, \"crickets\" were placed at the family level, but contemporary authorities including Otte now place them in the superfamily Grylloidea. The word has been used in combination to describe more distantly related taxa in the suborder Ensifera, such as king crickets and mole crickets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Gryllus_campestris_MHNT.jpg/330px-Gryllus_campestris_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket_(insect)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg/250px-Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg/250px-Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A medieval \"club ball\" game involving an underarm bowl towards a batter. Ball catchers are shown positioning themselves to catch a ball. Detail from the Canticles of Holy Mary, 13th century.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg/500px-Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg/500px-Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Evolution of the cricket bat. The original \"hockey stick\" (left) evolved into the straight bat from c. 1760, when pitched delivery bowling began.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org